WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.108
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4401.

2
00:00:02.108 --> 00:00:04.216
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:04.216 --> 00:00:05.622
<v KAT DONNER>Can you . . . ?

4
00:00:05.622 --> 00:00:09.135
It’s like on my neck.

5
00:00:09.135 --> 00:00:11.946
It’s a weird shirt.

6
00:00:11.946 --> 00:00:15.459
It’s not working for me.

7
00:00:15.459 --> 00:00:16.162
Okay.

8
00:00:16.162 --> 00:00:26.000
Alright so this interview’s probably going to take about ten to fifteen minutes.

9
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:26.328
<v JUDY MONTGOMERY GAUTHIA>Okay.

10
00:00:26.328 --> 00:00:28.625
<v DONNER>Maybe a little longer, a little less.

11
00:00:28.625 --> 00:00:31.578
<v GAUTHIA>Oh, no. I’m in a . . . I’m pushed for time.

12
00:00:31.578 --> 00:00:31.906
<v DONNER>Okay.

13
00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:35.188
Okay we’ll try to make it as brief as possible.

14
00:00:35.188 --> 00:00:38.141
We just want to get some basic background information.

15
00:00:38.141 --> 00:00:38.469
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

16
00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:42.734
<v DONNER>And then we’ll probably be in touch with you to get another interview.

17
00:00:42.734 --> 00:00:43.062
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

18
00:00:43.062 --> 00:00:45.031
Yeah because I didn’t come prepared.

19
00:00:45.031 --> 00:00:46.344
<v DONNER>That is totally fine.

20
00:00:46.344 --> 00:00:48.969
You can count me down whenever you’re ready.

21
00:00:48.969 --> 00:00:49.625
<v UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE>We’re recording.

22
00:00:49.625 --> 00:00:49.953
<v DONNER>Okay.

23
00:00:49.953 --> 00:00:58.156
So today is January twenty-fourth, 2015 and I’m Kat Donner representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

24
00:00:58.156 --> 00:01:04.391
We’re working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

25
00:01:04.391 --> 00:01:11.281
And to kick that off, we’re holding Mossville Oral History Day here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

26
00:01:11.281 --> 00:01:15.547
I’m here today with Ms. Judy . . . How do you pronounce your last name?

27
00:01:15.547 --> 00:01:16.203
Can you . . .?

28
00:01:16.203 --> 00:01:16.859
<v GAUTHIA>Gauthia [Go-shay].

29
00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:17.188
<v DONNER>Gauthia?

30
00:01:17.188 --> 00:01:18.172
<v GAUTHIA>Judy Montgomery Gauthia.

31
00:01:18.172 --> 00:01:18.500
<v DONNER>Okay.

32
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.797
And is that just your full name?

33
00:01:20.797 --> 00:01:22.766
<v GAUTHIA>Well Montgomery is my maiden name.

34
00:01:22.766 --> 00:01:23.094
<v DONNER>Okay.

35
00:01:23.094 --> 00:01:23.422
Alright.

36
00:01:23.422 --> 00:01:28.344
So like I said we’re just going to do a really brief fifteen minute interview.

37
00:01:28.344 --> 00:01:29.000
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

38
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:35.863
<v DONNER>We’ll probably get in touch with you for a follow up in the future.

39
00:01:35.863 --> 00:01:36.353
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

40
00:01:36.353 --> 00:01:41.745
<v DONNER>But just to start off, when and where were you born?

41
00:01:41.745 --> 00:01:42.725
<v GAUTHIA>Lafayette, Louisiana.

42
00:01:42.725 --> 00:01:43.216
<v DONNER>Okay.

43
00:01:43.216 --> 00:01:44.196
And when?

44
00:01:44.196 --> 00:01:46.157
<v GAUTHIA>1950 June the . . . fourteenth.

45
00:01:46.157 --> 00:01:46.647
<v DONNER>Okay.

46
00:01:46.647 --> 00:01:49.588
And what were your parents’ names?

47
00:01:49.588 --> 00:01:51.549
<v GAUTHIA>Eola and Valery Montgomery.

48
00:01:51.549 --> 00:01:52.039
<v DONNER>Okay.

49
00:01:52.039 --> 00:01:54.000
And your grandparents?

50
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.267
<v GAUTHIA>Abraham Montgomery and Mary Montgomery.

51
00:01:56.267 --> 00:01:58.987
And [Aidas?] Bernard and Jermaine Bernard.

52
00:01:58.987 --> 00:01:59.441
<v DONNER>Okay.

53
00:01:59.441 --> 00:02:02.161
And what did you parents do?

54
00:02:02.161 --> 00:02:05.788
<v GAUTHIA>Well, my daddy did a lot of things.

55
00:02:05.788 --> 00:02:06.695
Let’s see.

56
00:02:06.695 --> 00:02:09.869
He worked at the plant at Conoco.

57
00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:12.136
He retired there a janitor.

58
00:02:12.136 --> 00:02:16.669
And he had a business, Paradise Club and dance hall,

59
00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:23.017
so . . . And then he was in a vacuum truck business and hydro-blasting cleaning service.

60
00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:25.737
So he was a busy body.

61
00:02:25.737 --> 00:02:26.191
<v DONNER>Okay.

62
00:02:26.191 --> 00:02:27.551
And your mother?

63
00:02:27.551 --> 00:02:32.085
<v GAUTHIA>And my mother just worked hard right along with him.

64
00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:34.805
She just wasn’t in the front.

65
00:02:34.805 --> 00:02:36.619
She was the pusher.

66
00:02:36.619 --> 00:02:39.339
She’d push him, “Come on Valery.

67
00:02:39.339 --> 00:02:40.246
Let’s go.

68
00:02:40.246 --> 00:02:41.153
Come on.

69
00:02:41.153 --> 00:02:43.419
Got to get this done.”

70
00:02:43.419 --> 00:02:44.780
<v DONNER>Back bone there?

71
00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.953
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah she was the hard background worker.

72
00:02:47.953 --> 00:02:51.127
She worked hard right along with him.

73
00:02:51.127 --> 00:02:56.114
But she worked in the club and everything morning and night.

74
00:02:56.114 --> 00:02:58.835
<v DONNER>And where was the club located?

75
00:02:58.835 --> 00:03:00.195
<v GAUTHIA>On Prater Road.

76
00:03:00.195 --> 00:03:00.648
<v DONNER>Okay.

77
00:03:00.648 --> 00:03:02.008
<v GAUTHIA>41 Prater Road.

78
00:03:02.008 --> 00:03:02.462
<v DONNER>Okay.

79
00:03:02.462 --> 00:03:07.449
<v GAUTHIA>It was a route back then, but it’s on Prater Road.

80
00:03:07.449 --> 00:03:10.623
<v DONNER>So what brought your family to Mossville?

81
00:03:10.623 --> 00:03:18.784
<v GAUTHIA>I’m assuming they wanted to get out the country because they was from around Lafayette, Duson, Scott [Louisiana].

82
00:03:18.784 --> 00:03:27.852
And I done forgot how the story went, but seemed like to me they borrowed . . . forty dollars from his brother-in-law?

83
00:03:27.852 --> 00:03:29.665
And they came here.

84
00:03:29.665 --> 00:03:41.000
I done forgot exactly how it went, but they came here with forty dollars, a horse, and a mule it seemed like to me.

85
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:41.447
<v DONNER>Wow.

86
00:03:41.447 --> 00:03:42.341
<v GAUTHIA>Back then.

87
00:03:42.341 --> 00:03:48.600
<v DONNER>And do you remember what the property was like whenever you were growing up?

88
00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.624
Just the . . . Your house or the land around it?

89
00:03:52.624 --> 00:03:55.753
Could you just describe it for me?

90
00:03:55.753 --> 00:03:58.435
<v GAUTHIA>Well, it was a wooden house.

91
00:03:58.435 --> 00:04:00.671
And we had good neighbors.

92
00:04:00.671 --> 00:04:07.376
Rose and Sam Bernard was one of our neighbors, and the son was Eugene Fontenot.

93
00:04:07.376 --> 00:04:09.612
And we had the Mayos.

94
00:04:09.612 --> 00:04:13.635
<c.p>[Pushem?] Henry Mayo and Louise Mayo and their family.

95
00:04:13.635 --> 00:04:17.212
And [Emelle?] and Margie Peters and the Cotlong.

96
00:04:17.212 --> 00:04:19.894
Thelma Cotlong and Mr. Alvin Cotlong.

97
00:04:19.894 --> 00:04:22.576
I think that was his name.

98
00:04:22.576 --> 00:04:27.047
And my. . . A lot of people lived with my parents.

99
00:04:27.047 --> 00:04:30.176
My parents had always an open house.

100
00:04:30.176 --> 00:04:34.647
So they had a lot of people always stayed there.

101
00:04:34.647 --> 00:04:38.671
His brother lived with my dad a long time.

102
00:04:38.671 --> 00:04:39.565
Wes Montgomery.

103
00:04:39.565 --> 00:04:48.953
But he had a lot of brothers to live with us, so we always had a houseful and stuff like that.

104
00:04:48.953 --> 00:04:49.400
<v DONNER>Okay.

105
00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:50.741
Well . . . Let’s see.

106
00:04:50.741 --> 00:04:57.000
Where were some of the places that you liked to frequent around town?

107
00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.835
<v GAUTHIA>I liked to go to the swimming pool.

108
00:04:59.835 --> 00:05:07.633
Half the time Momma didn’t care for us to go there because she always thought everybody was going to drown I guess.

109
00:05:07.633 --> 00:05:12.241
And then they had the baseball games right out here in the field.

110
00:05:12.241 --> 00:05:14.013
And sometime we’d come there.

111
00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:19.329
I ain’t going to tell you I played baseball because I was a benchwarmer [laughs].

112
00:05:19.329 --> 00:05:21.810
<v DONNER>And what schools did you go to?

113
00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:25.000
<v GAUTHIA>Mossville was the only school I ever went to.

114
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:25.354
<v DONNER>Okay.

115
00:05:25.354 --> 00:05:28.544
<v GAUTHIA>I was the last class to graduate in 1969.

116
00:05:28.544 --> 00:05:28.899
<v DONNER>Okay.

117
00:05:28.899 --> 00:05:32.443
And can you just describe your experience there for me?

118
00:05:32.443 --> 00:05:38.114
<v GAUTHIA>I guess it was fun going to school, playing, looking forward to getting out of school.

119
00:05:38.114 --> 00:05:42.722
And once you got out of school, everything was a drag after that.

120
00:05:42.722 --> 00:05:45.557
I didn’t realize that was the best days.

121
00:05:45.557 --> 00:05:49.101
Being with a lot of people at school every day.

122
00:05:49.101 --> 00:05:49.456
But . . .

123
00:05:49.456 --> 00:05:50.873
<v DONNER>And what about church?

124
00:05:50.873 --> 00:05:53.000
Where’d you go to church?

125
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:53.597
<v GAUTHIA>Westlake.

126
00:05:53.597 --> 00:05:56.580
Saint John Bosco Church, Westlake.

127
00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:57.177
Catholic.

128
00:05:57.177 --> 00:05:57.773
<v DONNER>Okay.

129
00:05:57.773 --> 00:06:05.530
And who were some of the, if you can recall, the community leaders?

130
00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:12.094
<v GAUTHIA>I would say my dad was one of them, Valery Montgomery.

131
00:06:12.094 --> 00:06:13.287
Wes Montgomery.

132
00:06:13.287 --> 00:06:14.481
Butch Lemelle.

133
00:06:14.481 --> 00:06:16.271
Edward Julia Lemelle.

134
00:06:16.271 --> 00:06:23.431
Oh, there’s a lot of them, but I can’t remember them all.

135
00:06:23.431 --> 00:06:29.398
Dave Prince, he was with the team . . . With mean green.

136
00:06:29.398 --> 00:06:31.188
And Dorothy Felix.

137
00:06:31.188 --> 00:06:36.558
And his wife kind of promoted him, Diane Prince.

138
00:06:36.558 --> 00:06:39.541
She had cancer and died.

139
00:06:39.541 --> 00:06:45.508
And she was kind of following the team with them.

140
00:06:45.508 --> 00:06:55.652
Giving him her side of living near the plant because she was on Seven Avenue and stuff.

141
00:06:55.652 --> 00:06:58.635
So that’s how that was.

142
00:06:58.635 --> 00:07:05.796
<v DONNER>And let’s see . . . Okay so what does the community mean to you?

143
00:07:05.796 --> 00:07:12.359
<v GAUTHIA>Well I guess it’s home and I’ve never really left home.

144
00:07:12.359 --> 00:07:22.503
I live in Bigwoods in . . . near Edgerly on Highway 90, but to me Mossville was always home.

145
00:07:22.503 --> 00:07:36.227
I always stuck with my parents and I don’t know I just thought that was what I was supposed to do I guess,

146
00:07:36.227 --> 00:07:39.807
an old fashioned type of person.

147
00:07:39.807 --> 00:07:41.000
And . . .

148
00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:47.347
<v DONNER>So I guess just to take that a step further, what does home mean to you?

149
00:07:47.347 --> 00:07:48.140
<v GAUTHIA>It’s quiet.

150
00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:54.091
You can leave your door open and pretty much nobody going to mess with you.

151
00:07:54.091 --> 00:08:01.628
They might come in and help they self to some food or whatever because there’s always an open door.

152
00:08:01.628 --> 00:08:05.595
Half the time, ninety percent of the times it’s open.

153
00:08:05.595 --> 00:08:12.339
So my parents always took people in and I guess I’m going . . . A little follower and stuff.

154
00:08:12.339 --> 00:08:13.926
My dad’s still here.

155
00:08:13.926 --> 00:08:18.686
My momma been gone four years, but Daddy is eighty-nine right now.

156
00:08:18.686 --> 00:08:21.066
He’s not walking and stuff, but . . .

157
00:08:21.066 --> 00:08:24.636
<v DONNER>And does he still own that same property here?

158
00:08:24.636 --> 00:08:27.017
<v GAUTHIA>Yes, he still have his place.

159
00:08:27.017 --> 00:08:27.413
<v DONNER>Wow.

160
00:08:27.413 --> 00:08:27.810
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

161
00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.000
<v DONNER>That’s great.

162
00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:29.409
<v GAUTHIA>Yeah.

163
00:08:29.409 --> 00:08:31.451
Can’t wait to sell it.

164
00:08:31.451 --> 00:08:38.396
We in the process of wanting to sell it, and excited to be able to sell, so.

165
00:08:38.396 --> 00:08:43.706
Keep the plant going and I think that’s good they taking this over.

166
00:08:43.706 --> 00:08:49.017
And I’m glad to see they doing this history for Mossville, you see.

167
00:08:49.017 --> 00:08:49.426
<v DONNER>[Agrees].

168
00:08:49.426 --> 00:08:53.102
It’s very important to get your stories written down.

169
00:08:53.102 --> 00:08:58.004
But what sort of changes have you seen growing up in Mossville?

170
00:08:58.004 --> 00:09:03.723
<v GAUTHIA>We . . . Like my dad, he helped them do the sewage and water I guess.

171
00:09:03.723 --> 00:09:07.400
He tried to help do a lot of things.

172
00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:17.204
I can remember he was working at the plant, at Conoco, and I think him and his brother Wes Montgomery, if I remember right,

173
00:09:17.204 --> 00:09:27.826
they had went to the TV and they had told them how they was going to pass the water and the sewage and stuff like that.

174
00:09:27.826 --> 00:09:29.051
<v DONNER>That’s really interesting.

175
00:09:29.051 --> 00:09:33.545
<v GAUTHIA>So I guess he was a little help to the community.

176
00:09:33.545 --> 00:09:34.770
A big help.

177
00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:35.587
<v DONNER>That’s awesome.

178
00:09:35.587 --> 00:09:36.404
<v GAUTHIA>And stuff.

179
00:09:36.404 --> 00:09:42.940
So that’s why I’m happy to be able to sell my property to Sasol and everything.

180
00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:47.026
And happy that everybody else can go ahead and sell.

181
00:09:47.026 --> 00:09:50.294
I haven’t sold yet, but I am ready.

182
00:09:50.294 --> 00:09:58.055
Just waiting for a clearance with Road Home and once I get that done, I’ll be ready to sign.

183
00:09:58.055 --> 00:10:00.506
<v DONNER>And where do you live now?

184
00:10:00.506 --> 00:10:05.000
<v GAUTHIA>I’m staying with my dad; taking care of my dad.

185
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:05.452
<v DONNER>Okay.

186
00:10:05.452 --> 00:10:06.809
<v GAUTHIA>Staying with him.

187
00:10:06.809 --> 00:10:12.234
<v DONNER>And just a few questions just to sort of wrap things up,

188
00:10:12.234 --> 00:10:23.537
what is the most important thing for you that you feel people need to remember about Mossville that is not recorded in the history books?

189
00:10:23.537 --> 00:10:30.319
<v GAUTHIA>Just . . . my dad he would have loved to be able to give y’all his history,

190
00:10:30.319 --> 00:10:38.910
and he’s not able to because he has Alzheimer’s right now, so he can remember real good back then.

191
00:10:38.910 --> 00:10:40.718
And he loved politics.

192
00:10:40.718 --> 00:10:42.074
He loved that.

193
00:10:42.074 --> 00:10:48.856
I can remember him telling Momma how he’d rather see about that than to eat.

194
00:10:48.856 --> 00:10:53.830
She’d tell him, “You didn’t take time to eat yet, Valery.

195
00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.638
Come on and eat.”

196
00:10:55.638 --> 00:10:57.899
She loves to feed everybody.

197
00:10:57.899 --> 00:11:00.612
She likes for people to eat.

198
00:11:00.612 --> 00:11:02.872
And he’d say, “Not now.

199
00:11:02.872 --> 00:11:06.037
Not now let me get this done.

200
00:11:06.037 --> 00:11:11.915
I don’t want to forget to do . . .” whatever he was trying to do.

201
00:11:11.915 --> 00:11:20.053
So he liked to see about everything that was going on in the community and around the areas.

202
00:11:20.053 --> 00:11:21.862
Lake Charles and everywhere.

203
00:11:21.862 --> 00:11:22.766
<v DONNER>That’s great.

204
00:11:22.766 --> 00:11:24.122
That’s very kind.

205
00:11:24.122 --> 00:11:30.000
Well is there anything that you would want to add in closing?

206
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.231
<v GAUTHIA>No. not right now.

207
00:11:31.231 --> 00:11:34.000
<v DONNER>Did you have a chance to scan any documents?

208
00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:35.846
<v GAUTHIA>No I didn’t bring nothing today.

209
00:11:35.846 --> 00:11:37.385
I didn’t come prepared to . . .

210
00:11:37.385 --> 00:11:38.000
<v DONNER>That’s fine.

211
00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:43.231
If you . . . I was just going to let you know that if you just go through your . . .

212
00:11:43.231 --> 00:11:44.462
<v GAUTHIA>Pictures and stuff, yeah.

213
00:11:44.462 --> 00:11:50.308
<v DONNER>And your father’s things and you want to scan some of them for the record, we could do that.

214
00:11:50.308 --> 00:11:50.615
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

215
00:11:50.615 --> 00:11:54.000
<v DONNER>Just since we can’t actually get in touch with him.

216
00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:54.353
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

217
00:11:54.353 --> 00:11:58.235
And I can give you my cousin’s name and number for . . .

218
00:11:58.235 --> 00:11:58.588
<v DONNER>Please.

219
00:11:58.588 --> 00:12:07.059
<v GAUTHIA>Well, especially Danine. [Vanita?] I don’t know if she . . . I know Danine would be a . . . one that would be interested in giving they history.

220
00:12:07.059 --> 00:12:07.765
<v DONNER>Okay great.

221
00:12:07.765 --> 00:12:13.765
Well if you don’t have anything to add we’re going to go ahead and stop the recording.

222
00:12:13.765 --> 00:12:14.118
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

223
00:12:14.118 --> 00:12:15.882
Yes you can go ahead.

224
00:12:15.882 --> 00:12:18.353
I thought we had already stopped it.

225
00:12:18.353 --> 00:12:19.059
<v DONNER>I’m sorry.

226
00:12:19.059 --> 00:12:19.765
<v GAUTHIA>That’s okay.

227
00:12:19.765 --> 00:12:23.294
<v DONNER>And we’re just going to burn that CD for you.

228
00:12:23.294 --> 00:12:24.000
<v GAUTHIA>Okay.

229
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.077
<v DONNER>But let me just get that information from you.

230
00:12:26.077 --> 00:12:26.308
<v GAUTHIA>Alright.

231
00:12:26.308 --> 00:12:27.000
I’m looking.

232
00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:27.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4401.

233
00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:27.200
End Session I.]
